   ],
   "notes": [
    "Updated notes: the present now has the fuller endings ـين (you f.) and ـون (you pl., they): تشترين، تشترون، يشترون. Earlier notes had the shorter تشتري / تشترو. The full forms are the common Gulf / Saudi ones.",
    "Spelling: شروا and شريتوا end in a silent alif, which is the formal convention. Both spellings occur and sound the same. Likewise شرا and شرى are the same word.",
    "بيشتري (huwa biyishtiri hadiyya) is your teacher’s second way to talk about the future; with a time word it reads “he will buy”. راح is the clearer marker.",
    "Command: m. and f. are the same word (اشتري) because the verb ends in ي. In the plural the final ن drops: تشترون → اشتروا.",
    "The short past شرى and the longer present يشتري come from the same root ش-ر-ي. اشترى is also heard for the past in many dialects. The Fusha column was added for reference; it was not on your sheets."
   ]
